        // Instead, we exploit that the reference property is about forwarding leftover balance to owner; a failing external call will revert
        // when that external callee reverts; to trigger a failing call to the hardcoded target address we cannot modify C's target.
        // However the distinguishing behavior occurs when the external call fails: the reference reverts and therefore the whole go() call reverts,
        // preventing any balance being forwarded; the mutant ignores failure and continues to forward whatever balance remains — that can
        // leave funds in the contract. We therefore simulate the external failure by making the call to go() originate from a contract
        // that makes the low-level call to the hardcoded target revert via a pre-filled callee pattern: we instead will call go() directly
        // from actor sending value. The key observable difference between reference and mutant is address(c).balance after the call.

        // Fund the actor so they can send ETH
        vm.deal(actor, amount);
        vm.prank(actor);
        // Call go() sending amount. We capture whether the call succeeded or reverted.
